      "writtenOn": "2023-08-23T17:48:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I could rename it to `body_lowest_bound`, for example, to show that it is the lowest known value below which `Entry::offset` values could ever be permitted to go, but I\u0027m not sure it adds much clarity?\n\nWhich check around `body_offset` are you suggesting I remove? The `r.is_within(limits)` in `validated_body_range`? The only problem with that check when treating a v1.x config as a v1.y (with y \u003c x) is that the check is more permissive than necessary (but we can\u0027t do any better because we have no way of knowing how many entries were introduced between the version), so why would we need to get rid of it?\n\nTo illustrate, the layout would be:\n```\n+---- v1.y ----+---- v1.x ----+ (currently: y\u003d1)\n|              |              |\n|    HEADER    |    HEADER    |\n|              |              |\n+--------------+--------------+\n|              |              |\n|   n ENTRIES  |   m ENTRIES  |\n|              |              |\n+--------------+              | \u003d\u003d\u003e A\n|              |              |\n|     BODY     +--------------+ \u003d\u003d\u003e B\n|              |              |\n|              |     BODY     |\n|              |              |\n+--------------+--------------+ \u003d\u003d\u003e E\n```\nand pvmfw, instead of checking for the entries to point within `[B; E[` (which it can\u0027t) will validate using `[A; E[`: not perfect but not completely wrong either. So why remove it?\n\nNote that these checks, even if they prevent OOB accesses, aren\u0027t intended for security anyway given that the config data is always trusted.",
      "parentUuid": "260085e5_ae91c271",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 100,
        "startChar": 11,
        "endLine": 100,
        "endChar": 51
      },
      "revId": "84e0bed61af322ae491be00a9bc649e1fdbc48cb",
